# Traefik routing smoke check for @APP@. With a base_domain in the /compose
# payload, af-api stops publishing each app's host port and routes it through
# Traefik at @APP@.$BASE_DOMAIN instead. Every container can therefore be
# healthy on the internal docker network while nothing is reachable from outside:
# a broken Traefik render leaves the container health checks fully green, so this
# is the only block that catches it.
#
# --resolve rather than -H "Host: ...": it sets SNI and the Host header to the same
# name, so the check keeps routing if sniStrict is ever turned on, and it exercises
# the URL a real client would use. -k stays either way -- af-test.invalid can never
# obtain an ACME certificate, so Traefik answers with its self-signed default cert.
#
# Asserted as a regex over the status line, not a fixed code, because several
# unrelated codes all prove the same thing:
#   pass  2xx, 3xx (any redirect), 401, 403 -- Traefik matched a router for this
#         host and forwarded upstream. Whether the app then demands a login is
#         not this test's business.
#   fail  404 -- Traefik is listening but has no router for this host, the usual
#         symptom of a bad render.
#   fail  5xx -- a router exists but the upstream did not answer. Not strictly a
#         routing bug, and test_health_check_@APP@ should catch it first, but
#         there is no reading of a 5xx here that is good news.
#   fail  000 -- nothing is listening on :443 at all. curl also exits non-zero
#         there, so expected_exitcode = 0 catches it a second way (and covers
#         TLS-level failures, which never produce a status code).
[test_traefik_route_@APP@]
class = Plugins.Ssh.Test
executors = exec_vm
cmd = curl -k -sS -o /dev/null -w "traefik route @APP@ http_code=%{http_code}\n" --max-time 10 --resolve @APP@.$BASE_DOMAIN:443:127.0.0.1 https://@APP@.$BASE_DOMAIN/
expected_stdout = http_code=(2[0-9][0-9]|3[0-9][0-9]|401|403)$
comparator_stdout = regex
expected_exitcode = 0
